L1 gate — rotation and crash-resume
2026-08-10 · TimeLakeDB at :1963 · agent run inside the database's
network namespace · corpus and checkpoint on a container-local
filesystem (see THE RIG below — this turned out to matter enormously)
================================================================================

ROTATION — 120,000 lines, rename-and-recreate every 30,000
  Four rotations executed while the agent was following the file, each
  one moving the live inode to a sibling name and creating a new one.

  agent:  read=120000 shipped=120000 quarantined=0 rotations=4 files_lost=0
  gate:   rows 120000 · distinct 120000 · min 0 · max 119999 · EXACT

  The bytes written between the agent's last read and each rename exist
  only in the old inode; the drained-before-followed rule is what keeps
  them, and the exact count is what proves it.

CRASH-RESUME — 600,000 lines, SIGKILL mid-stream
  Killed with 92,000 of 600,000 rows shipped — a real mid-stream death,
  not a clean shutdown. Checkpoint at the moment of death:

    {"files":[{"dev":131,"ino":1788852,"offset":9826539}],
     "last_tick_ns":1786322945807000000,"next_seq":10}

  Note `next_seq: 10` — the kill landed *inside* a millisecond, ten
  lines into it. Restoring only the offset would have restarted that
  millisecond's sequence at zero and overwritten those ten rows. This is
  the exact window DESIGN.md §3.2 exists for, and the checkpoint carries
  the stamper state precisely so it cannot happen.

  After restart: rows 600000 · distinct 600000 · min 0 · max 599999 · EXACT

  Both halves hold: nothing lost (the restart resumed at the right byte)
  and nothing duplicated (the replayed lines regenerated identical
  timestamps and collapsed under last-write-wins instead of doubling).

TWO BUGS THE DRILL FOUND, BOTH REAL
  1. The agent DIED during rotation. `check_rotation` reopened the path
     with `?`, and every rotation has a window between the rename and
     the recreate where the path does not exist. So a routine logrotate
     run killed the process — exactly when it must not die. It now
     treats a missing path as "look again next poll"; the drained file
     is already safe by then.
  2. SIGTERM was not handled, only ctrl-c. systemd and Kubernetes both
     stop a process with SIGTERM, so every ordinary restart would have
     dropped the in-flight batch. Both signals now flush and exit.

  A unit test also caught an ordering bug before the drill ran: rotation
  was detected *after* the drain check, so the new file's first line was
  emitted before the old file's tail. Sequence numbers follow emission
  order, so that mattered.

THE RIG — a Docker Desktop bind mount cannot test rotation at all
  The first three rotation runs failed at 4,000 / 7,714 / 6,549 rows,
  logging "file truncated" on what were plainly renames. Instrumenting
  the check showed why:

      file truncated  len=0  offset=375884  ino=30117822508470618

  An open fd whose file has been renamed reported **length 0** instead
  of its real size, and the inode numbers were 17-digit synthetic values.
  On a Docker Desktop host mount the POSIX guarantee the tailer depends
  on — an open descriptor keeps addressing its file across a rename —
  does not hold. Moving the corpus to a container-local path made all
  four rotations work first time.

  This is the same shape as TimeLakeDB's port-forwarding discovery: the
  measurement rig was lying, and the code was fine. Recorded here so the
  next person does not spend the same hour. **Rotation drills must not
  run on a bind mount**; the checkpoint directory is kept off it too,
  because fsync there is slow enough that the agent falls behind its own
  source and a slow run looks like data loss.

  The drill also now waits for quiescence — polling until the row count
  stops advancing — rather than sleeping a fixed interval, for the same
  reason: a slow run must not be mistaken for a lossy one.

WORKSPACE
  cargo test --workspace: 27 passed, 0 failed
    + tail: 7 (growing file, partial line, rename-and-recreate drains
      first, copy-and-truncate, resume mid-file, resume after a rotation
      that happened while stopped, vanished file counted)
    + checkpoint: 3 (round-trip incl. stamper state, no temp litter,
      lookup by identity)

NOT CLAIMED AT L1
  The disk queue, backpressure beyond honouring Retry-After,
  bisect-on-400, multiline, watermarks. All L2.
